   165	
   166	## 3. Theorem CI-SERIES: the length, derived combinatorially
   167	
   168	Let `D_t(s) = (1-s) prod_{j=2}^{t-1}(1-s^j) (1-s^{t+1})` be the weighted
   169	denominator of `S_t`, and let the tail degrees be `2t+2, .., 3t+1`.
   170	
   171	**Theorem CI-SERIES.**  For every `t>=2`,
   172	
   173	```text
   174	P_t(s) := prod_{d=2t+2}^{3t+1}(1-s^d) / D_t(s) = (1+s^{t+1}) * [3t+1 choose t-1]_s .
   175	```
   176	
   177	*Proof.*  Split off the first numerator factor and use the Gaussian binomial in
   178	the form `[n choose k]_s = prod_{d=n-k+1}^{n}(1-s^d) / prod_{i=1}^{k}(1-s^i)`
   179	with `n = 3t+1`, `k = t-1`:
   180	
   181	```text
   182	prod_{d=2t+3}^{3t+1}(1-s^d) = [3t+1 choose t-1]_s * prod_{i=1}^{t-1}(1-s^i),
   183	D_t(s) = prod_{i=1}^{t-1}(1-s^i) * (1-s^{t+1}),
   184	```
   185	
   186	so `P_t(s) = [3t+1 choose t-1]_s * (1-s^{2t+2})/(1-s^{t+1})
   187	= (1+s^{t+1}) [3t+1 choose t-1]_s`, because `2t+2 = 2(t+1)`.  QED
   188	
   189	Three consequences, all immediate and all requested by card item (2):
   190	
   191	* **Exactness and nonnegativity.**  `P_t` is a polynomial with nonnegative
   192	  coefficients, since Gaussian binomials are.  No cancellation argument is
   193	  needed; the "numerator-denominator cancellation" is the single identity
   194	  `(1-s^{2t+2})/(1-s^{t+1}) = 1+s^{t+1}`.
   195	* **Degree and symmetry.**  `deg P_t = (t-1)(2t+2) + (t+1) = (2t-1)(t+1)`, and
   196	  `P_t` is palindromic (Gorenstein, as a CI must be).
   197	* **The length, combinatorially.**  Conjugating partitions in the
   198	  `(t-1) x (2t+2)` box turns `[3t+1 choose t-1]_s` into the weight-generating
   199	  function of the monomials in `t-1` variables of weights `1,..,t-1` whose
   200	  TOTAL (unweighted) degree is at most `2t+2`.  Hence
   201	
   202	```text
   203	[s^n] P_t = #{ (eps,m) : eps in {0,1}, m in Z_{>=0}^{t-1}, |m|_1 <= 2t+2,
   204	                          eps(t+1) + sum_i i*m_i = n },
   205	L_t = P_t(1) = 2 * binom((2t+2)+(t-1), t-1) = 2 * binom(3t+1, t-1)
   206	            = prod_{d=2t+2}^{3t+1} d / ((t-1)! (t+1)) = (t/(t+1)) binom(3t+1,t).
   207	```
   208	
   209	`ci_series_identity.py` verifies exactness of the division, the Gaussian
   210	identity, nonnegativity, the degree, the binomial form of `L_t` and the
   211	combinatorial model (I6) for `t=2..16` in exact integer arithmetic; all pass
   212	(`ALL_IDENTITY_CHECKS_PASS = True`).  The equality of `2 binom(3t+1,t-1)` with
   213	the product form `prod_{d=2t+2}^{3t+1} d / ((t-1)!(t+1))` is the elementary
   214	rewrite `binom(3t+1,t-1) = (3t+1)!/((t-1)!(2t+2)!)` and `2t+2 = 2(t+1)`; it is
   215	confirmed numerically against every banked measurement below.
   216	
   217	| `t` | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 10 |
   218	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
   219	| `L_t` | 14 | 90 | 572 | 3640 | 23256 | 149226 | 961400 | 6216210 | 40320150 |
   220	| measured | exact, new here (`y=2/5`) | exact (banked); mod `p` both fibres here | exact (banked); mod `p` both fibres here | mod `p`, both fibres (banked) | mod `p`, both fibres (banked) | mod `p`, one fibre (banked) | -- | -- | -- |
   221	
   222	`P_3 = [1,1,2,2,4,4,6,6,8,7,8,7,8,6,6,4,4,2,2,1,1]` (degree 20, sum 90).
   223	
   224	**What this does and does not do.**  It closes the length half of the card
   225	completely and it fixes the target series.  It does *not* bear on dimension:
   226	`P_t` is the series the quotient *has if* the tail is an hsop.  The logical
   227	direction is `dim = 0  =>  length = L_t`, never the converse; Theorem
   228	DEGREE-BLIND (Sec. 6) shows that this direction cannot be reversed by any
   229	degree-only reasoning.
